  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nCurrently, isolated thunderstorms were located over south\ncentral ND. This is within an area of 2000 J/kg of instability\naloft with moderate to strong effective shear. The latest WV\nimagery and upper level heights show an upper level trough over\nthe Pacific Northwest with a mean upper level ridge over central\nMontana. A speed max was located over eastern Montana with south\ncentral ND in the left front quad of the shortwave. This is\nenough forcing to continue to develop isolated to scattered\nthunderstorms over south central ND, with the instability aloft.\n\nFor today, as this speed max pushed east through the state,\nthink there may be a continued threat of showers and \nthunderstorms through the day. SPC has placed a marginal risk of\nsevere storms mainly along and south of the Highway 200 \ncorridor today. Can't really argue with this as the one thing \nthat seems most certain this morning is the instability today is\nforecast to be much lower north of the Highway 200 corridor. \nThere may still be showers or a few thunderstorms, but right now\nforecast soundings don't support severe storms in the north. As\nyou get south of the Highway 200 corridor, things get muddled \npretty quickly. \n\nThe southwest and south central look to be the most unstable\nthis afternoon with 1500-3000 J/Kg of MUCape. Shear is\nsufficient at around 40 knots, but increases as you go north\nfrom southern into central and northern ND. There are a few\nlimiting factors. With the aforementioned shortwave there will \nbe more clouds around today. The RAP also indicates the \npotential for some stratus today over the southern half of the \nCWA, which could limit instability. In general, the instability \nmay not be as great as yesterday, when we had highs in the upper\n80s and dew points in the mid and upper 60s in southwest and \nsouth central ND. Today we are expecting highs in the lower to \nmid 80s with dew point in the low to mid 60s. However, \ninstability will be sufficient and with the moderate to strong \nshear, we will have the potential for strong to severe storms \nthis afternoon into this evening. The main question is where and\nwhen. With the shortwave moving through we see continued \nconvection over the south central early this morning, and this \nmay spread north and east today. Then as the RRQ of the \nshortwave moves across the area later today, this may provide \nthe forcing for ascent needed to initiate convection. The \ninterface of the strong instability to the south and the strong \nshear to the north would be an ideal area for severe convection \ntoday. The main hazards being large hail to golfball size and \nwinds to 60 mph, if we can get a sustained updraft. The far \nsouthwest will be the most unstable and convection developing \nover southeast Montana may move into southwest ND. The \nenvironment would be favorable for quick development with steep \nlapse rates. With a broad southwest upper flow, it's hard to say\nwhere and when convection may develop and CAMS are bouncing \naround a bit. Nevertheless, there will be a threat for strong to\nsevere storms today. \n\nOnce convection ends today/this evening, we cool down a bit for\nThursday and especially Friday. We remain in a favorable\nsouthwest upper flow over the far west on Thursday, then\neventually across the area Friday, but with less instability,\nthe threat for strong to severe storms will be low. Currently\nSPC has only general thunderstorms forecast for Thursday and\nFriday. After Friday we begin a slow warming trend through the\nweekend and into next week. Saturday and Sunday may end up being\nmostly dry before near daily chances for thunderstorms return\nearly next week.",
